{
  "teamId": 109,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 Arizona Diamondbacks were a team that defied expectations from the very first swing of the season. Kyle Lewis announced Arizona's intentions on Opening Day with a two-run blast, and the Diamondbacks raced through April and May at a combined 32-22 clip, surfing a wave of timely hitting and clutch relief performances. Andrew Chafin surrendered a walk-off shot in May but was also on the mound for some of the team's most dramatic victories, and the bullpen's boom-or-bust nature became a defining characteristic of a roster that could beat you one night and break your heart the next.\n\nJune kept the good times rolling at 16-11, as Christian Walker's gap-splitting doubles and the lineup's collective depth made Arizona one of the NL's most dangerous clubs. Then July arrived like a desert thunderstorm — the team went a dismal 8-16, the worst month of the season, as relievers like Miguel Castro and Andrew Chafin gave up crushing home runs in high-leverage spots and the offense went uncharacteristically quiet. August was nearly as rough at 12-15, a stretch where even bright individual moments — Walker's power, Ketel Marte's clutch hits — couldn't paper over a team that seemed to be fading from postseason contention.\n\nSeptember became a rallying cry. The Diamondbacks went 15-12 to claw back into the Wild Card picture, and when October arrived, something clicked. Arizona stormed through the postseason bracket as a fifth seed, dispatching the Brewers, Dodgers, and Phillies in remarkable fashion to reach the World Series — the franchise's first Fall Classic since 2001. The October run was electric, defined by Corbin Carroll's youthful fearlessness, the infield's defensive wizardry, and a bullpen that finally held when it mattered most.\n\nThe World Series against the Texas Rangers proved a bridge too far. Paul Sewald, a reliable closer all year, surrendered a Corey Seager two-run bomb in Game 1 that swung momentum decisively toward Texas, and the Diamondbacks never fully recovered. Miguel Castro was tagged for an Adolis Garcia homer in the same contest, and Arizona fell in five games. Yet the defeat could not diminish what this team accomplished — an 84-win club that played its best baseball when the stakes were highest, giving a desert city its most memorable October in over two decades.",
  "chapters": [
